import assertions from '../../components/relational-next/a12/assertions.sql?raw';
const sections = [
 ['start-with-the-questions-the-past-must-answer', 'Start with the questions the past must answer'],
 ['store-the-facts-each-question-needs', 'Store the facts each question needs'],
 ['separate-an-entity-from-its-versions', 'Separate an entity from its versions'],
 ['define-time-boundaries-and-correction-rules', 'Define time boundaries and correction rules'],
 ['decide-what-leaving-the-active-set-means', 'Decide what leaving the active set means'],
 ['test-what-the-history-can-truthfully-establish', 'Test what the history can truthfully establish'],
];
const lookup = `SELECT version_no, fee_eur
FROM customer_rates
WHERE tenant_id = 1 AND customer_id = 7
  AND effective_from <= date '2026-11-10'
  AND (effective_to IS NULL
       OR date '2026-11-10' < effective_to);
-- version_no | fee_eur
--          2 |   22.00`;
const versionReference = `ALTER TABLE bookings\n  ADD UNIQUE (tenant_id, booking_id, customer_id);\n\nCREATE TABLE rate_uses (
  tenant_id integer NOT NULL,
  booking_id integer NOT NULL,
  customer_id integer NOT NULL,
  version_no integer NOT NULL,
  PRIMARY KEY (tenant_id, booking_id),
  FOREIGN KEY (tenant_id, booking_id, customer_id)
    REFERENCES bookings (tenant_id, booking_id, customer_id),
  FOREIGN KEY (tenant_id, customer_id, version_no)
    REFERENCES customer_rates
      (tenant_id, customer_id, version_no)
);`;
const recordedLookup = `SELECT fee_eur
FROM rate_assertions
WHERE tenant_id = 1 AND customer_id = 7
  AND daterange(effective_from, effective_to, '[)')
      @> date '2026-11-11'
  AND tstzrange(recorded_from, recorded_to, '[)')
      @> timestamptz '2026-11-11 12:00+00';
-- 20.00
-- Change only the recording cutoff to
-- '2026-11-12 10:00+00': the answer becomes 22.00.`;
---

<p>A current row tells you the latest stored state. It does not tell you what a customer agreed, which rate applied last Tuesday or what the system knew when it issued a report. Keeping history starts by choosing which of those answers the application must preserve.</p>
<p>We will extend the class-booking service from <a href="/review/relational-next/data-design/">Designing relational data</a>. Mina is customer C7 in tenant 1; her booking B11 retains a €20.00 agreement. The parent already separates that agreement from today’s offer. Here we will preserve more of the past only where a concrete question needs it, then follow those facts when Mina leaves.</p>

<nav class="article-contents" aria-label="In this article"><ol>{sections.map(([id,title]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{title}</a></li>)}</ol></nav>

<h2 id="start-with-the-questions-the-past-must-answer">Start with the questions the past must answer</h2>
<p>Mina changes her address after booking. Staff correct a room assignment. She cancels and later reactivates B11. A contractual rate change arrives two days late. “Show the old data” is too vague to decide what to store through those changes.</p>
<table><caption>Different questions need different retained facts</caption><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Fact to preserve</th><th>Smallest useful representation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Where should we contact Mina now?</td><td>Her current contact address</td><td>Current profile row</td></tr>
 <tr><td>What address and fee did B11 agree?</td><td>The agreement at confirmation</td><td>Booking snapshot</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Was B11 cancelled and then reactivated?</td><td>Successful changes in order</td><td>Committed change records</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Which rate applied on 11 November?</td><td>A rate’s period of applicability</td><td>Effective-dated versions</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Why did the 11 November report show €20?</td><td>The account recorded before a late correction</td><td>Effective and recorded time, or the issued report itself</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Give each requirement an owner and a retention period. The booking team owns the agreement; reporting owns whether a past report must be explained or reproduced exactly. Keeping a report’s original file may answer that last requirement more directly than versioning every source table. A current profile is sufficient where no older profile answer is promised.</p>
<p>Decide what a correction means too. “Blue Room was entered by mistake; it should always have been Green Room” revises our account of the past. “The class moves from Blue to Green next week” changes what applies from next week. Both can arrive as an update to a room field, but they make different historical claims.</p>

<h2 id="store-the-facts-each-question-needs">Store the facts each question needs</h2>
<p>Current state, snapshots, change records and effective-dated versions can coexist. They have different row meanings. Treating all four as an undifferentiated history table makes it difficult to know which question a query actually answers.</p>

<h3 id="preserve-an-agreement-with-a-snapshot">Preserve an agreement with a snapshot</h3>
<p>Suppose B11’s confirmation now agrees a billing address as well as a fee. A <strong>snapshot</strong> captures those agreed details on the booking. It is not a copy that should follow every later profile edit: “address agreed for B11” and “C7’s current address” are different facts.</p>
<p>In Figure 1, confirm B11, change Mina’s profile and read the booking again. At the final step, switch from <em>Agreed details</em> to <em>Current reference</em>. Watch which address the old booking can establish.</p>
<HistoryFigure kind="snapshot" number={1} />
<p>The snapshot boundary is the agreement, not the whole customer row. Retain the fee, currency meaning, agreed address and terms needed to explain the confirmation. Copying unrelated profile details creates more information to correct, protect and eventually remove.</p>
<p>Capture the values the customer actually accepted at the confirmation boundary. If an offer changes between display and confirmation, silently reading the new offer into B11 does not establish acceptance. The booking transaction must validate or identify the accepted offer and store the agreement together. A later authorised amendment needs its own meaning and evidence; ordinary profile maintenance must not rewrite it. <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/">Transactions</a> owns the competing-change protocol, and <a href="/review/relational-next/normalisation/">normalisation</a> distinguishes these historical facts from derived copies that should stay current.</p>

<h3 id="record-changes-when-the-sequence-matters">Record changes when the sequence matters</h3>
<p>A cancelled status says B11 is cancelled now. It cannot distinguish one cancellation from three cancellation–reactivation cycles. A <strong>change record</strong> retains a change as a separate fact. First choose whether it records an attempted action, a before-and-after difference or the complete resulting state.</p>
<p>Figure 2 records complete resulting statuses. Step through a rejected cancellation and a successful one, then reactivate. At the end, compare the retained representations.</p>
<HistoryFigure kind="changes" number={2} />
<p>Requests alone cannot reconstruct committed state: a request can fail, be duplicated or have no effect. Full resulting-state records can answer “status after revision 2” if they begin with an initial state and capture every relevant committed change. Difference records instead need a starting state and enough information to apply each change unambiguously. A log of statuses cannot reconstruct an old address that it never recorded.</p>
<p>Use a per-booking revision number with uniqueness on <code>(tenant_id, booking_id, revision_no)</code>, alongside the recorded time, action, actor identity and reason where required. Allocate revisions through the protected update protocol, not an unprotected “maximum plus one”. Revisions define the order for this booking; an unrelated generated identifier or wall-clock timestamp need not define commit order across bookings. In PostgreSQL, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/functions-datetime.html#FUNCTIONS-DATETIME-CURRENT"><code>current_timestamp</code></a> is fixed at transaction start, so multiple changes can receive the same value.</p>
<p>Commit the state change and its successful-change record together. Writing the record in a later request can leave a cancellation with no history after a crash. Triggers or a controlled write interface can capture changes, but every import, repair and staff tool must use the chosen mechanism. An attempted-action audit may deliberately survive a failed change; keep its meaning distinct from the committed-state sequence.</p>

<h3 id="use-effective-dated-versions-for-facts-with-a-period-of-applicability">Use effective-dated versions for facts with a period of applicability</h3>
<p>Mina’s organisation now negotiates a customer rate for future bookings. The rate is €20 from 1 November and €22 from 10 November. Keeping the time each row was inserted does not by itself say when either rate applies. An <strong>effective-dated version</strong> states that period explicitly.</p>
<p>The following extension runs after the parent’s schema and fixture. It uses business dates: the contract changes by calendar day in the organisation’s agreed calendar. It does not model an intraday change. Each row means “this customer’s rate applies from this date up to, but excluding, its end”.</p>
<details><summary>Customer rate versions and the two-row fixture</summary><Sql code={rates} label="One scoped customer identity, several non-overlapping rate versions" /></details>
<p>A future version can be stored before it applies. A late version can be stored after it first applied. Therefore “last inserted”, “greatest version number” and “applicable today” are different selections. A booking may use the applicable contractual rate to form a new agreement; it still retains its own accepted amount. Correcting a rate does not silently amend existing bookings.</p>

<h2 id="separate-an-entity-from-its-versions">Separate an entity from its versions</h2>
<p>C7 identifies Mina’s enduring customer record. Rate version <code>(1, 7, 2)</code> identifies one stored version for that customer. Neither the rate’s value nor its effective start is a good substitute for a version identity when corrections may change those attributes.</p>
<p>A reference needs a declared meaning. B11’s customer reference supports today’s roster. A reference to a specific rate version supports explaining which rate was selected. A booking snapshot preserves what was actually agreed, including any approved exception. Those references can all be useful on the same booking.</p>
<Sql code={versionReference} label="An optional record of which customer rate a booking used" />
<p>The additional booking key lets the first reference enforce that this is the booking’s customer. The second reference identifies that customer’s complete tenant–customer–version target. These references do <em>not</em> enforce that the rate applied at confirmation or that its fee equals the agreement. Those require additional rules and a protected capture path. <a href="/review/relational-next/constraints/#reference-the-whole-relationship">Expressing rules with constraints</a> develops complete composite relationships. Do not mistake two valid references for proof that they describe the same business relationship.</p>
<p>A version reference preserves values only if that version’s descriptive payload is retained under a defined correction policy. Overwriting version 1’s fee changes every report following it. For fixed historical evidence, keep the version payload, add a superseding assertion, or store the required snapshot. Retain tenant scope in both identity and version keys; version 2 for another customer is not this version 2.</p>

<h2 id="define-time-boundaries-and-correction-rules">Define time boundaries and correction rules</h2>
<p>“As of 10 November” needs a precise meaning: an effective business date, or information recorded by a particular instant? Define the requested axis, boundary and missing-result behaviour before choosing a query.</p>

<h3 id="make-each-instant-resolve-to-a-defined-version">Make each instant resolve to a defined version</h3>
<p>Use inclusive starts and exclusive ends, written <code>[start, end)</code>. Version 1 ends at 10 November; version 2 starts there. The boundary belongs to version 2 once. For a timestamp-based fact, use the same convention with resolved instants. Do not subtract a second or a microsecond to manufacture an end: that ties correctness to storage precision.</p>
<p>In Figure 3, move the date across the handover. Keep it on 10 November and choose <em>Overlap</em>, then <em>Gap</em>. Count the versions that claim the same date.</p>
<HistoryFigure kind="periods" number={3} />
<Sql code={lookup} label="Ask for the rate on the exact effective boundary" />
<p>Our nullable end means “no effective end recorded”, not “the end is unknown”. The predicate spells that out. PostgreSQL’s <code>daterange</code> constructor represents this null upper bound as unbounded; the <code>[)</code> argument chooses the boundary convention. The row checks forbid zero-length and reversed periods. <a href="/review/relational-next/types/#store-the-time-the-application-actually-means">Choosing types</a> owns dates, instants and time-zone resolution.</p>
<p>The exclusion constraint rejects overlapping periods for the same tenant and customer. It enforces <em>at most one</em> applicable version. It does not enforce <em>at least one</em>: a gap is still possible. Our service requires continuous coverage from the customer’s contract start, so replacement writers must preserve adjacency and handle failures as one transaction. A query finding no rate must report that missing prerequisite; falling back silently to the latest rate invents applicability.</p>
<p>A read spanning 9–11 November crosses two rates and should return or split the relevant periods, rather than pretend one point lookup answers the whole interval. For a half-open requested interval, overlap means the version starts before the requested end and its end is absent or after the requested start. A non-overlap rule for one customer’s versions is separate from preventing two customers booking the same room. The <a href="/review/relational-next/constraints/#make-reservation-conflicts-a-stored-rule-where-possible">constraints article</a> owns enforcement mechanisms; <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/">transactions</a> owns safe multi-row replacement.</p>

<h3 id="add-recorded-time-only-when-corrections-require-it">Add recorded time only when corrections require it</h3>
<p>On 11 November the system still knows only €20. It issues a report. At 10:00 UTC on 12 November, staff learn that €22 should have applied from 10 November. Updating the effective periods now gives the corrected account of 11 November. It loses the earlier account unless that too is retained.</p>
<p>Figure 4 keeps the effective date at 11 November. Play the late change, then switch the recording cutoff at the last step.</p>
<HistoryFigure kind="correction" number={4} />
<p><strong>Effective time</strong> says when the rate applies. <strong>Recorded time</strong> says when a stored assertion belongs to the system’s recorded account. Keeping both is commonly called <strong>bitemporal</strong> modelling. The extra axis is justified here by two required answers, not by a preference for keeping more dates.</p>
<table><caption>The retained assertions after the late change</caption><thead><tr><th>Fee</th><th>Effective dates</th><th>Recorded period, UTC</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>€20</td><td>1 November onwards</td><td>1 November 09:00 to 12 November 10:00</td></tr>
 <tr><td>€20</td><td>1 November to 10 November</td><td>12 November 10:00 onwards</td></tr>
 <tr><td>€22</td><td>10 November onwards</td><td>12 November 10:00 onwards</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>All periods use exclusive ends. At the correction boundary, the first assertion leaves the recorded account and the replacement pair enters it. That pair supersedes the earlier account over its entire effective coverage: it preserves €20 before 10 November and corrects the later portion. Deleting the old assertion would prevent the earlier-cutoff query.</p>
<details><summary>Schema and fixture for these three assertions</summary><Sql code={assertions} label="Retain the superseded account as well as the replacement" /></details>
<Sql code={recordedLookup} label="Hold effective date fixed and change the recorded cutoff" />
<p>The native fixture supplies assertion times explicitly; it does not implement automatic capture or claim these are measured commit times. A production design must define who sets recorded time, which changes share a boundary and how readers observe committed assertions consistently. Its invariant is no two conflicting assertions for one customer at the same effective and recorded point. The fixture’s exclusion rule prevents overlap on both axes together; coverage and the replacement transaction remain separate obligations.</p>
<p>Automatically maintained row history may preserve when stored values changed without knowing their business applicability. Check its documented semantics against these questions. Reproducing the exact issued report also requires its original query or calculation version, parameters and every relevant input, or the report output itself. Two rate axes alone cannot recover a report’s old customer name or formatting.</p>

<h2 id="decide-what-leaving-the-active-set-means">Decide what leaving the active set means</h2>
<p>Cancelling B11 changes the booking’s business status. Marking Mina inactive changes whether the service accepts new activity for her. Neither statement means that her profile or agreement has been erased.</p>
<p>A <strong>tombstone</strong> retains a minimal enduring identity and departure state after descriptive details are removed. Archiving moves details to a less available storage or access path. Deleting a row removes it from the live relation. Choose among them from the remaining questions: can B11 still be explained, may Mina return, which details may remain and when must each copy leave?</p>

<h3 id="follow-references-uniqueness-and-reports-through-removal">Follow references, uniqueness and reports through removal</h3>
<p>Figure 5 follows the same retained B11 through alternative lifecycle policies. The tombstone and archive choices assume a schema designed to support those shapes. They are design alternatives, not updates that can all run against the parent’s required profile columns and references.</p>
<HistoryFigure kind="removal" number={5} />
<p>An inactive row still satisfies an ordinary foreign key and participates in an unfiltered join. Ordinary uniqueness still reserves its membership code. If codes may be reused, define uniqueness over the intended active set and define what reactivation does when another customer owns the code. <a href="/review/relational-next/constraints/#enforce-uniqueness-only-where-the-rule-applies">Conditional uniqueness</a> develops that rule. Never reuse C7’s enduring identity for a new person: old bookings would then point to the newcomer.</p>
<p>A minimal identity allows B11 to remain, displaying “departed customer” where a current name is no longer retained. Removing a name from the profile does not remove it from confirmation snapshots. If a report filters out inactive customers before joining, it may lose B11 and undercount historical bookings. Filter by booking status for a current roster; use the report’s own lifecycle rule for historical agreements.</p>
<p>Moving a referenced parent row out of its table is not transparent to its foreign key. Under the parent’s <code>ON DELETE RESTRICT</code>, the move’s deletion is refused. An archive design can retain the stable identity in the referenced table and move only the profile payload, or redesign the related storage and access path. An archived copy still exists and needs access and retention rules. A unique index on the live profile alone cannot reserve codes held only in a separate archive.</p>
<p>Cascading deletion deliberately removes dependent rows; it would destroy the retained booking requirement here. Setting a reference to null preserves a booking row but removes its direct customer link. Those can be legitimate policies elsewhere. They are consequences to choose, not settings to add merely to make deletion succeed.</p>

<h3 id="specify-retention-and-erasure-beyond-the-row">Specify retention and erasure beyond the row</h3>
<p>Write a removal requirement that operations can implement. For example: “After departure, retain B11’s amount and non-identifying session facts until the approved agreement-retention deadline. Remove current contact details and their copies by the approved removal deadline. Retain only the identity needed for permitted references, with restricted access. A restore must reapply completed removals before the service exposes restored data.” The deadlines and permitted evidence are explicit policy inputs; this example does not choose jurisdictional requirements.</p>
<table><caption>Each copy needs its own lifecycle decision</caption><thead><tr><th>Copy</th><th>Required decision</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Current profile and historical versions</td><td>Which fields are removed, minimised or retained, and by when</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Agreement snapshots and issued reports</td><td>Which evidence must remain; whether identifying details can be removed</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Derived results, exports and search indexes</td><td>Who removes or rebuilds each copy and verifies completion</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Replicas and backups</td><td>How removal propagates, retained-copy expiry and how restoration prevents reappearance</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>A surrogate identifier is not proof of anonymity: B11, an export or a retained mapping may still identify Mina. Deleting a PostgreSQL row also does not overwrite every physical copy; old row versions await storage cleanup, while backups and exports have independent lives. Treat database deletion, access removal and the required erasure outcome as separately testable obligations.</p>
<p><a href="/review/relational-next/operations/">Running a relational service</a> owns access, removal and restoration procedures. <a href="/review/relational-next/live-changes/">Changing a live database</a> owns converting required profile columns, references and existing data to the chosen lifecycle shape. Keep those procedures grounded in the model’s retention and reporting promises.</p>

<h2 id="test-what-the-history-can-truthfully-establish">Test what the history can truthfully establish</h2>
<p>Walk one booking through the whole lifecycle. Confirm B11 at €20 with 4 Elm Street; edit the profile to 9 Oak Road; record the late rate change; cancel, reactivate and eventually remove contact details. Write the expected answers before running the queries.</p>
<ul>
 <li>The current profile changes address. The agreement remains €20 and 4 Elm Street until its own amendment or removal policy changes it.</li>
 <li>The rate for 9 November is €20. At the 10 November boundary it becomes €22. A query spanning the boundary encounters both versions.</li>
 <li>For effective date 11 November, the earlier recorded cutoff returns €20; the correction cutoff returns €22. A date before the first effective version returns no rate.</li>
 <li>Cancellation removes B11 from the active roster. Reactivation restores it without hiding the retained committed sequence.</li>
 <li>A departed-customer report retains B11 under the approved identity policy. Its permitted profile details, snapshots and exports follow their separate removal requirements.</li>
</ul>
<p>The sequential rate and reference examples were executed on PostgreSQL 18.6. Boundary queries and the two recorded cutoffs returned the amounts above. Overlaps, empty or reversed periods, missing version targets and cross-tenant version targets were rejected. A gap was accepted: that is evidence that non-overlap alone does not establish coverage. These checks do not establish a concurrent replacement protocol or complete removal procedure.</p>
<p>Finally, ask who can change the evidence. Rows containing an actor name do not authenticate that actor. History captured only by one application path can omit direct writes. An administrator able to update the history can rewrite it. Restrict write authority, define capture and attribution, and decide whether independent audit storage is required. The <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/#limit-who-can-reach-and-change-the-data">operations article</a> owns those controls. Retained rows establish what this capture mechanism recorded under its assumptions, not that every real-world action happened exactly as written.</p>
<p>Keep current rows for present facts, snapshots for agreements, changes for required sequences and effective periods for applicability. Add recorded time when earlier knowledge is itself a required answer. That gives each retained fact a purpose and gives its eventual removal a requirement that can be tested.</p>

